      "result": {
        "reply_text": "I understand that you purchased shoes from CloudBasket for Rs 2400, the order was cancelled, and you have not received your refund despite contacting their support team.\n\nE-commerce entities are required to process refunds for requests made according to the rules within a reasonable period of time. Sellers on a marketplace are prohibited from refusing to refund consideration paid if services or goods are deficient.\n\nYou may register your grievance through the National Consumer Helpline (NCH) at 1915 or via their website (consumerhelpline.gov.in). This is a pre-litigation stage for grievance redressal. If the issue remains unresolved, you may file a complaint with the District Commission, provided the value of the goods does not exceed the prescribed limits. A complaint must generally be filed within two years from the date the cause of action arose.\n\nKeep copies of your invoice, the cancellation email, and your bank statement as proof of your transaction and the failed refund. If you decide to approach the District Commission, you may need to provide these documents to support your claim.\n\nIf you are unable to resolve the matter through the National Consumer Helpline or if the company continues to ignore your requests, you may consider consulting a lawyer to understand the formal process for filing a consumer complaint.\n\nSource: Consumer Protection (E-Commerce) Rules, 2020, rule 4\nSource: Consumer Protection (E-Commerce) Rules, 2020, rule 6\nSource: National Consumer Helpline Grievance Redressal Procedure, procedure 1\nSource: National Consumer Helpline Guidelines, helpline 1915\nSource: Consumer Protection Act, 2019, section 34\nSource: Consumer Protection Act, 2019, section 69\nSource: Consumer Protection (Jurisdiction of the District Commission, the State Commission and the National Commission) Rules, 2021, rule 3\n\nWhat I could not verify:\n- where to complain if the District Commission process is not suitable for your specific situation\n\nThis is legal information, not legal advice.",
